{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-132284",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$  \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=22$ and $n=31$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(22,31)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,31).$ To find the inverse of $22\\,(\\text{mod}\\,31)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm. \n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}31 & = & 22\\cdot 1 & + & 9 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 22 & = & 9\\cdot 2 & + & 4 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 9 & = & 4\\cdot 2 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =31-22 \\\\ 4 & =22-9\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =9-4\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =9-4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9-(22-9\\cdot 2)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9\\cdot 5-22\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(31-22)\\cdot 5-22\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =31\\cdot 5-22\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =31\\cdot 5+22\\cdot (-7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have \n\n$$\n1=31\\cdot 5+22\\cdot (-7).\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $31,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}31\\cdot 5+22\\cdot (-7) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ 22\\cdot (-7) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ 22\\cdot (31-7) & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ 22\\cdot 24 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $22\\cdot 24≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,31),$ which means that $24$ is the multiplicative inverse of $22$ (modulo $31$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $24,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}22x & ≡5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ 24\\cdot 22x & ≡24\\cdot 5 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ (24\\cdot 22)x & ≡120 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡3\\cdot 31+27 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31) \\\\ x & ≡27 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,31)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,31).$",
